          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.111,
            0.655,
            0.13
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the volume of a cone? It turns out that"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.441,
            0.142,
            0.556,
            0.177
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_9_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "V   =   \\frac { 1 } { 3 }   \\pi r ^ { 2 } h ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.179,
            0.883,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The volume formula tells us that the volume of a cone is exactly "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the volume of a cylinder formed on the same circular base and with the same height as the cone. The formula may be expressed in different ways, e.g.,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.343,
            0.262,
            0.654,
            0.297
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_9_equation_5.jpg",
          "content": "V = { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } } \\times { \\mathrm { a r e a ~ o f ~ b a s e } } \\times { \\mathrm { h e i g h t } } ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.295,
            0.882,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Students often wonder about the factor of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in this formula. Why "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " ? To explain the reason for this factor requires more advanced methods, e.g., calculus, which you will study when you are in Grade 12."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.367,
            0.883,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "But it is possible to experimentally verify this relationship. Let us start with a right circular cylindrical tin. Using paper, we make a right circular cone with the same base radius and the same height as the cylinder (see Fig. 14.10)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.459,
            0.876,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.121,
                0.459,
                0.876,
                0.642
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_9_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.451,
                0.657,
                0.549,
                0.676
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 14.10"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.685,
            0.882,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We are now going to perform an experiment to find the volume of the cone. We need some clean fine-grained salt for this purpose."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.724,
            0.883,
            0.839
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fill the cone to the brim with salt. Empty it into the cylinder. We find that it fills up only part of the cylinder. Fill the cone with salt to the brim again and empty it into the cylinder. We find that the cylinder is still not full. When we fill the cone with salt for the third time and empty it yet again into the cylinder, we find that the cylinder is full to the brim."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.838,
            0.882,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can therefore conclude that the volume of the cylinder is three times the volume of the cone. That is, given a right circular cylinder and"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
